<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement CCCL on NCCL 2.28.3 with 1020 lines of code.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Evaluation Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware.</span>
We use three nodes, each equipped with an Intel(R) Xeon(R) 6960P CPU (72 physical cores), 256 GB of DRAM, and one NVIDIA H100 GPU with 80 GB device memory, connected through a PCIe Gen5<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>16 link. All three nodes connect to a TITAN-II CXL switch using PCIe/CXL Gen5<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>16 links. Behind the switch, a memory box provides a CXL shared memory pool using six Micron CZ120 CXL Type-3 memory cards, each with 128 GB capacity and a PCIe/CXL Gen5<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>8 interface, for a total pool size of 768 GB. We disable the DDIO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> to avoid using LLC for the data transfer and set the memory pool address space as non-cacheable area. The CXL shared memory pool is not available in the market. Our evaluation platform, using realistic hardware (not emulation or simulation), represents the-start-of-the-art.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Software.</span> All nodes run Linux 6.2.6 with CUDA 12.8.
We use nccl-tests 2.17.8 for evaluation. <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ncclSendRecv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ncclSendRecv</annotation></semantics></math> are not tested because they are point-to-point primitives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline.</span> We use Infiniband with 200 Gb/s as the baseline. All collective communication primitives are tested with variable message sizes, ranging from 1MB to 4GB. We categorize the primitives into two types: <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to 1 (or 1 to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>). Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1. Evaluation Setup ‣ 4. Evaluation ‣ CCCL: Node-Spanning GPU Collectives with CXL Memory Pooling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the primitives we test.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We have three implementations of CCCL: (1) CCCL-ALL, which is a full-featured implementation of CCCL; (2) CCCL-Aggregate has bandwidth aggregation but the aggregation occurs at a coarse-granularity. There is no “asynchrony and overlapping”. (3) CCCL-Naive allocates memory in the memory pool sequentially without using memory interleaving. This is the default memory allocation policy in operating system. There is no “asynchrony and overlapping”.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">NCCL primitives. <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the buffer size per rank, and <span id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nranks</span> is the number of participating ranks.</span></figcaption>
